 extendable $(n,2,d)_{p^2/p}$-codes, p prime, always admit additive extensions.\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nOn the Maximality of Additive Codes\n\nT. L. Alderson∗\n\nAbstract\n\nAn additive (n, k, d)q m/q-code is a GF(q)-linear subspace of GF(q m)n of\n\nGF(q)-dimension km with minimum Hamming distance d. We ﬁrst extend the\n\nAlderson–Bruen–Silverman (ABS) model of linear codes to the additive setting:\n\na code of length n with q km words over an alphabet of size q m admits an ABS\n\nmodel if and only if it is equivalent to a nondegenerate additive code. We then\n\nask whether an additive code that admits an extension must admit an additive\n\nextension. For linear codes (m= 1) this is a theorem of Alderson and G´acs.\n\nWe characterize the additive codes admitting no additive extension as those\n\nwhose associated projective system of ﬂats is complete, and we prove that the\n\nanswer to the question above is again aﬃrmative for (n, 2, d)9/3-, (n, 2, d)4/2-,\n\nand (n, 3, d)4/2-codes. In contrast with the linear case, we show that the answer\n\nis negative in general. Scattered linear sets yield, for each square q, extendable\n\nadditive (n, 2, d)q 2/q-codes admitting no additive extension. Further, a diﬀerent\n\nmethod yields an extendable additive (30,2,24)8/2-code with no additive exten-\n\nsion. Consequently, for properly additive codes, completeness of the associated\n\nprojective system does not imply maximality of the code. We conjecture that\n\nextendable (n, 2, d)p 2/p-codes, p prime, always admit additive extensions.\n\nKeywords: additive codes, code extensions, maximal codes, projective systems,\n\ndirections in aﬃne spaces, scattered linear sets\n\nMSC 2020: Primary 94B05, 51E22; Secondary 94B27, 51E20, 51E21\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSets of $p^2$ points in $\\mathrm{AG}(4,p)$ determining no complete line of directions, and extendable additive codes without additive extensions_国家预印本平台 (https://sinoxiv.napstic.cn/article/26343681)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Weighting each line by its number of undetermined directions turns every such graph into an extendable additive code without an additive extension; applied to Alderson's examples, the weighting answers a question of his: the analogue of (ii) fails over every non-prime field. ... finite geometry/direction sets/additive codes/blocking sets/SAT solving/verified proof checking\n\n### 摘要\n\nAlderson conjectured that for every prime $p$ (i) every set of $p^2$ points of $\\operatorname{AG}(4,p)$ determines all points of some line of $\\operatorname{PG}(3,p)$, and (ii) every extendable additive $(\\nu,2,d)_{p^2/p}$ code admits an additive extension. Over non-prime fields $\\mathbb F_q$ the analogue of (i) fails, and that of (ii) for square $q$; over prime fields (i) was known only for $p\\le3$. We show that both parts fail for infinitely many primes, and at the first open prime $p=5$ we settle the case of graphs of maps $\\mathbb F_p^2\\to\\mathbb F_p^2$. Our counterexamples are product graphs built from base-$\\lceil p^{1/3}\\rceil$ digits. For every prime $p>4804^3$ their direction sets contain no complete line, by a lattice-point estimate uniform in the slope and a quotient-set argument. Evaluating an exact criterion by computer shows that they already work for every prime $29\\le p<20000$ except twenty primes below $223$; the smallest of them, $841$ points in $\\operatorname{AG}(4,29)$, can be checked by hand. Weighting each line by its number of undetermined directions turns every such graph into an extendable additive code without an additive extension; applied to Alderson's examples, the weighting answers a question of his: the analogue of (ii) fails over every non-prime field. At $p=5$ every $25$-point graph determines a complete line: symmetry and elementary counting reduce this to $50$ propositional formulas, whose unsatisfiability is certified by the formally verified checker cake_lpr. A counterexample at $p=5$ would leave between $55$ and $103$ directions undetermined. Hence the least primes violating (i) and (ii) lie between $5$ and $29$.\n\n### Abstract\n\nAlderson conjectured that for every prime $p$ (i) every set of $p^2$ points of $\\operatorname{AG}(4,p)$ determines all points of some line of $\\operatorname{PG}(3,p)$, and (ii) every extendable additive $(\\nu,2,d)_{p^2/p}$ code admits an additive extension. Over non-prime fields $\\mathbb F_q$ the analogue of (i) fails, and that of (ii) for square $q$; over prime fields (i) was known only for $p\\le3$. We show that both parts fail for infinitely many primes, and at the first open prime $p=5$ we settle the case of graphs of maps $\\mathbb F_p^2\\to\\mathbb F_p^2$. Our counterexamples are product graphs built from base-$\\lceil p^{1/3}\\rceil$ digits. For every prime $p>4804^3$ their direction sets contain no complete line, by a lattice-point estimate uniform in the slope and a quotient-set argument. Evaluating an exact criterion by computer shows that they already work for every prime $29\\le p<20000$ except twenty primes below $223$; the smallest of them, $841$ points in $\\operatorname{AG}(4,29)$, can be checked by hand. Weighting each line by its number of undetermined directions turns every such graph into an extendable additive code without an additive extension; applied to Alderson's examples, the weighting answers a question of his: the analogue of (ii) fails over every non-prime field. At $p=5$ every $25$-point graph determines a complete line: symmetry and elementary counting reduce this to $50$ propositional formulas, whose unsatisfiability is certified by the formally verified checker cake_lpr. A counterexample at $p=5$ would leave between $55$ and $103$ directions undetermined. Hence the least primes violating (i) and (ii) lie between $5$ and $29$.\n\n### 关键词\n\nfinite geometry/direction sets/additive codes/blocking sets/S